      <data key="d4">{"artifact_or_process_linked": "RFD framework tying natural-language requirements, logical requirements, SysML-style models, and simulations.", "claim_text": "RFD can produce high-confidence pico-/nano-satellite designs that are correct by construction, formally verified, and responsive to requirement changes.", "evidence_source": "\"Reliable and Formal Design (RFD) process whose results are correct by construction, formally verified, and responsive to system requirement changes.\"", "extractor_confidence": "high", "field_path": "claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[]", "observed_effect_or_qualitative_effect": "Claimed high-confidence, correct-by-construction, formally verified, change-responsive design; not empirically observed.", "quote_location": "Abstract", "source_claim_id": "C1", "source_codebook_version": "1.1", "source_generated_at": "2026-05-28T15:59:44-06:00", "source_path": "studies[7].extraction.claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[0]", "study_id": "paper-0010", "threats_or_alternative_explanations": "No PNSat/EPS case or outcome metric; correctness depends on complete and valid formalization of requirements and assumptions."}</data>
    </node>
    <node id="paper-0010::claim-02">
      <data key="d2">Integrating formal methods at appropriate design levels can eliminate many design failures and integration challenges.</data>
      <data key="d3">Claim</data>
      <data key="d4">{"artifact_or_process_linked": "Formal methods embedded in RFD abstraction levels.", "claim_text": "Integrating formal methods at appropriate design levels can eliminate many design failures and integration challenges.", "evidence_source": "\"By integrating formal methods into the proposed design process at the appropriate levels, many design failures and integration challenges can be eliminated.\"", "extractor_confidence": "high", "field_path": "claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[]", "observed_effect_or_qualitative_effect": "Claimed elimination of many failures/challenges; no observed effect reported.", "quote_location": "Section I Introduction", "source_claim_id": "C2", "source_codebook_version": "1.1", "source_generated_at": "2026-05-28T15:59:44-06:00", "source_path": "studies[7].extraction.claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[1]", "study_id": "paper-0010", "threats_or_alternative_explanations": "Physical interfaces, environmental effects, and incorrect assumptions may remain outside the formal model; no integration-test evidence is reported."}</data>
    </node>
    <node id="paper-0010::claim-03">
      <data key="d2">Correct-by-construction formal design enables virtual testing at each refinement level and may reduce system-integration testing burden.</data>
      <data key="d3">Claim</data>
      <data key="d4">{"artifact_or_process_linked": "RFD abstraction levels $A_i$, parametric simulations $S_p$, behavior simulations $S_b$, and formal checks.", "claim_text": "Correct-by-construction formal design enables virtual testing at each refinement level and may reduce system-integration testing burden.", "evidence_source": "\"The integration of formal methods into the design process of a complex system can reduce the need for a significant amount testing during the system integration phase because of the \"correct by construction\" nature of the process. This leads to testing virtually at each level of refinement.\"", "extractor_confidence": "high", "field_path": "claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[]", "observed_effect_or_qualitative_effect": "Claimed reduction in physical integration testing; not measured.", "quote_location": "Section I Introduction", "source_claim_id": "C3", "source_codebook_version": "1.1", "source_generated_at": "2026-05-28T15:59:44-06:00", "source_path": "studies[7].extraction.claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[2]", "study_id": "paper-0010", "threats_or_alternative_explanations": "Virtual tests are not shown for a satellite; no evidence of model fidelity, integration readiness, or residual physical-test needs."}</data>
    </node>
    <node id="paper-0010::claim-04">
      <data key="d2">SysML as a common MBSE language can improve cross-disciplinary communication and reduce ambiguity at interfaces and abstraction levels compared with document-centric practice.</data>
      <data key="d3">Claim</data>
      <data key="d4">{"artifact_or_process_linked": "SysML models/profiles/stereotypes used as the basis for small-satellite design process development.", "claim_text": "SysML as a common MBSE language can improve cross-disciplinary communication and reduce ambiguity at interfaces and abstraction levels compared with document-centric practice.", "evidence_source": "\"Another important aspect of SysML is that it facilitates communication by being a common language across organizational disciplines, thereby reducing ambiguity caused by a lack of a common formal design semantics.\"", "extractor_confidence": "high", "field_path": "claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[]", "observed_effect_or_qualitative_effect": "Claimed ambiguity reduction and communication support; no stakeholder evaluation.", "quote_location": "Section II.B Model Base Systems Engineering Design Process", "source_claim_id": "C4", "source_codebook_version": "1.1", "source_generated_at": "2026-05-28T15:59:44-06:00", "source_path": "studies[7].extraction.claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[3]", "study_id": "paper-0010", "threats_or_alternative_explanations": "Benefits depend on team adoption, modeling skill, profile quality, and governance; none are evaluated."}</data>
    </node>
    <node id="paper-0010::claim-05">
      <data key="d2">Formalizing requirements in higher-order logic and checking them in PVS can improve reliability/correctness by exposing contradictions and type-correctness proof obligations early.</data>
      <data key="d3">Claim</data>
      <data key="d4">{"artifact_or_process_linked": "Logical requirement set $L_l$, PVS theories/axioms, and type-correctness conditions.", "claim_text": "Formalizing requirements in higher-order logic and checking them in PVS can improve reliability/correctness by exposing contradictions and type-correctness proof obligations early.", "evidence_source": "\"By specifying a complex system in a mathematically rigorous format, it is possible to improve system reliability\"; Section V: \"this type checking condition (TCC) would have to be discharged successfully.\"", "extractor_confidence": "high", "field_path": "claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[]", "observed_effect_or_qualitative_effect": "Potential contradiction detection and reliability improvement; paper shows the form of a TCC but no completed satellite proof result.", "quote_location": "Sections II.C, IV.A, and V", "source_claim_id": "C5", "source_codebook_version": "1.1", "source_generated_at": "2026-05-28T15:59:44-06:00", "source_path": "studies[7].extraction.claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[4]", "study_id": "paper-0010", "threats_or_alternative_explanations": "Formal methods cannot correct wrong assumptions; PVS example is a railway crossing and the TCC is not reported as a satellite result."}</data>
    </node>
    <node id="paper-0010::claim-06">
      <data key="d2">Formal traceability across refinement levels lets engineers track requirement origin, importance, implementation, and testing status.</data>
      <data key="d3">Claim</data>
      <data key="d4">{"artifact_or_process_linked": "Abstraction levels $A_i$, transition-system refinements, refinement relation $\\Delta$, and PVS proof obligations.", "claim_text": "Formal traceability across refinement levels lets engineers track requirement origin, importance, implementation, and testing status.", "evidence_source": "\"Through traceability, each project engineer has access to contextual information that can assist in determining where a requirement came from, its importance, how it was implemented, and how it was tested.\"", "extractor_confidence": "high", "field_path": "claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[]", "observed_effect_or_qualitative_effect": "A formal traceability mechanism is formulated; implementation of the traceability check is future work.", "quote_location": "Section IV.B Traceability and Section VI Conclusion", "source_claim_id": "C6", "source_codebook_version": "1.1", "source_generated_at": "2026-05-28T15:59:44-06:00", "source_path": "studies[7].extraction.claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[5]", "study_id": "paper-0010", "threats_or_alternative_explanations": "No traceability tool implementation or trace-link dataset; authors note constraining refinements may limit design space."}</data>
    </node>
