    <node id="paper-0012::claim-01">
      <data key="d2">Analytical, model-based architecture enables rapid exploration of large CubeSat mission design spaces by avoiding repeated numerical orbit propagation.</data>
      <data key="d3">Claim</data>
      <data key="d4">{"artifact_or_process_linked": "MATLAB discipline model architecture; analytical trajectory/visibility methods; MOGA search.", "claim_text": "Analytical, model-based architecture enables rapid exploration of large CubeSat mission design spaces by avoiding repeated numerical orbit propagation.", "evidence_source": "\"These results were obtained in 10 minutes on a personal workstation... solution convergence after 102 generations (i.e 51,000 iterations of the model architecture).\" / \"This allows rapid, long-term visibility assessments to be made in fractions of a second (as opposed to minutes... using numerical approaches)\".", "extractor_confidence": "high", "field_path": "claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[]", "observed_effect_or_qualitative_effect": "Two-case optimization ran 51,000 model iterations in 10 minutes; conclusion claims visibility assessments in fractions of a second instead of minutes for numerical approaches.", "quote_location": "Section 4.2; Section 5 Conclusions", "source_claim_id": "C1", "source_codebook_version": "1.1", "source_generated_at": "2026-05-28T15:59:44-06:00", "source_path": "studies[9].extraction.claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[0]", "study_id": "paper-0012", "threats_or_alternative_explanations": "No independent runtime benchmark or accuracy validation versus numerical propagation; speed may depend on simplified mission assumptions and hardware."}</data>
    </node>
    <node id="paper-0012::claim-02">
      <data key="d2">The architecture finds Pareto sets of near-optimal CubeSat mission designs from user-defined design spaces.</data>
      <data key="d3">Claim</data>
      <data key="d4">{"artifact_or_process_linked": "Controlled elitist MOGA applied to trajectory, space, launch, ground, and performance modules; Pareto plots in Figures 4-7.", "claim_text": "The architecture finds Pareto sets of near-optimal CubeSat mission designs from user-defined design spaces.", "evidence_source": "\"Global multi-objective optimization... is applied to the architecture and is shown to find a Pareto set of near-optimal solutions from an arbitrary, user defined design space.\"", "extractor_confidence": "high", "field_path": "claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[]", "observed_effect_or_qualitative_effect": "Pareto sets are shown for GSD/revisit and data-return/cost trade studies; decision variables include altitude, inclination, and ground-station latitude.", "quote_location": "Abstract; Section 5 Conclusions", "source_claim_id": "C2", "source_codebook_version": "1.1", "source_generated_at": "2026-05-28T15:59:44-06:00", "source_path": "studies[9].extraction.claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[1]", "study_id": "paper-0012", "threats_or_alternative_explanations": "Case studies are synthetic/illustrative and Pareto fronts depend on model assumptions, penalty functions, and OTS database values."}</data>
    </node>
    <node id="paper-0012::claim-03">
      <data key="d2">The model exposes non-intuitive design trends that could be missed by point-design methods.</data>
