    <node id="paper-0022::claim-03">
      <data key="d2">Concept lifecycle trade studies can be structured by decomposing MOEs to MOPs/TPMs, identifying model elements/parameters, and interfacing the mission model with STK to quantify performance.</data>
      <data key="d3">Claim</data>
      <data key="d4">{"artifact_or_process_linked": "MOE/MOP/TPM decomposition, model-element parameter mappings, STK interface, state/parametric/activity diagrams.", "claim_text": "Concept lifecycle trade studies can be structured by decomposing MOEs to MOPs/TPMs, identifying model elements/parameters, and interfacing the mission model with STK to quantify performance.", "evidence_source": "Section III.C: 'First, the MOEs are decomposed into measures of performances (MOPs) and technical performance measures (TPMs).' 'The interface supports the input (driving parameters) into the space system model and the output (quantifying parameters) from the space system model.'", "extractor_confidence": "medium", "field_path": "claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[]", "observed_effect_or_qualitative_effect": "Qualitative asserted effect: establishes and explores a mission trade space using model-linked measures and simulation outputs.", "quote_location": "Section III.C Concept Life Cycle Trade Studies.", "source_claim_id": "C3", "source_codebook_version": "1.1", "source_generated_at": "2026-05-28T15:59:44-06:00", "source_path": "studies[14].extraction.claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[2]", "study_id": "paper-0022", "threats_or_alternative_explanations": "Workflow is described but no current example mission, numerical outputs, sensitivity analysis, or comparison to manual trade studies is shown."}</data>
    </node>
    <node id="paper-0022::claim-04">
      <data key="d2">Power/data/communications concept trades can evaluate whether candidate subsystem models provide sufficient power to collect and downlink mission data.</data>
      <data key="d3">Claim</data>
      <data key="d4">{"artifact_or_process_linked": "Candidate Data Acquisition and Handling, Power, and Communication subsystem models; second-order trade space.", "claim_text": "Power/data/communications concept trades can evaluate whether candidate subsystem models provide sufficient power to collect and downlink mission data.", "evidence_source": "Section III: 'The following two trade studies were demonstrated: 1) On board energy level as a function of solar panel area and maximum battery capacity, and 2) quantity of data downloaded...' Section III.C: 'The model is used to establish and evaluate a second-order trade space that provides sufficient power for collect and downlink mission data...'", "extractor_confidence": "medium", "field_path": "claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[]", "observed_effect_or_qualitative_effect": "Qualitative asserted effect: evaluates sufficient power for mission data collection/downlink; earlier RAX lineage demonstrated onboard-energy and downloaded-data trade studies.", "quote_location": "Section III background on prior phases; Section III.C.", "source_claim_id": "C4", "source_codebook_version": "1.1", "source_generated_at": "2026-05-28T15:59:44-06:00", "source_path": "studies[14].extraction.claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[3]", "study_id": "paper-0022", "threats_or_alternative_explanations": "The demonstrated trade studies are referenced from earlier papers rather than reported with methods/results here; 'sufficient power' is not quantified as an EPS verification result."}</data>
    </node>
    <node id="paper-0022::claim-05">
      <data key="d2">Phase-specific reference models can make MBSE adoption more gradual for CubeSat teams new to SysML/MBSE and ease model modification.</data>
      <data key="d3">Claim</data>
      <data key="d4">{"artifact_or_process_linked": "Lifecycle-phase model subsets derived from the CubeSat Reference Model.", "claim_text": "Phase-specific reference models can make MBSE adoption more gradual for CubeSat teams new to SysML/MBSE and ease model modification.", "evidence_source": "'Providing a fully-formed CubeSat Reference Model could be a bit overwhelming... Having models for each life-cycle phase is a more gradual way to get up to speed. That should also make it easier for a team to modify, add, and remove SysML elements...'", "extractor_confidence": "medium", "field_path": "claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[]", "observed_effect_or_qualitative_effect": "Qualitative asserted effect: less overwhelming adoption and easier add/remove/modify of SysML elements.", "quote_location": "Section III, after lifecycle-stage discussion.", "source_claim_id": "C5", "source_codebook_version": "1.1", "source_generated_at": "2026-05-28T15:59:44-06:00", "source_path": "studies[14].extraction.claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[4]", "study_id": "paper-0022", "threats_or_alternative_explanations": "No user study, training-time metric, or onboarding comparison is reported."}</data>
    </node>
    <node id="paper-0022::claim-06">
      <data key="d2">A properly designed reference model can reduce systems-engineering burden, serve as a checklist, and promote uniformity and consistency across future CubeSat models.</data>
