    <edge source="paper-0023::claim-02" target="outcomecategory::decision_support" id="e0000645">
      <data key="d5">CLAIMS_OUTCOME</data>
      <data key="d6">claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[].outcome_category</data>
      <data key="d7">decision support</data>
      <data key="d8" />
      <data key="d9">Section 3.1: "The project management procedures proposed by standards like ECSS are sometimes too over-specified for nanosatellite projects which are usually undertaken by universities or academic institutions." Section 3.2: academic nanosatellite specificities allow simplifying the structure: requirements are derived from mission objectives; budget is allocated to hardware/software; "schedule and Coast Breakdown Structure can be directly derived from the Product Tree"; "students-driven projects" have changing personnel and "Business Agreement Structure can be neglected." Section 3.4: outputs for the Formal Design Phase include "Product breakdown structure (PBS)", "Work breakdown structure (WBS)", and "Cost breakdown structure (CBS)"; CBS costs are primarily hardware/software development and testing for student-driven projects.</data>
      <data key="d10">high</data>
      <data key="d11">{"claim_id": "paper-0023::claim-02", "claim_text": "Tailoring ECSS/project-breakdown structures through MBSE simplifies academic nanosatellite project management by deriving schedule/CBS from the product tree and omitting industrial customer-supplier structures not needed for student projects.", "evidence_confidence": "Weak", "observed_effect": "Qualitative simplification of project structures for academic/non-customer-supplier contexts; no measured management improvement.", "source_claim_id": "C2", "source_path": "studies[15].extraction.claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[1]", "source_record_id": "paper-0023::claim-02", "study_id": "paper-0023"}</data>
    </edge>
