      <data key="d7">completeness</data>
      <data key="d8" />
      <data key="d9">Section 5.1: "requirements tables were generated ... These tables describe the requirements and their relationship to other SysML design elements to facilitate requirements traceability, satisfaction, and verification." Section 5.1/Fig. 15: "The "Satisfied By" column shows the model elements that fulfill the requirement. For example, the "Power System" subsystem must satisfy operational requirement 100.1.6."; "The 'Verified By' column shows the specific test cases that determine how the system meets the requirement ... "Bus System Activation" activity diagram ... is used to verify operational requirement 100.1.6." Section 5.1/Fig. 16: "The requirements matrix is another important SysML element that can be generated for requirements to design trace"; Fig. 16 shows a Mission Satisfy Requirement Matrix with Power System and Battery/Kill Switch rows.</data>
      <data key="d10">high</data>
      <data key="d11">{"claim_id": "paper-0023::claim-04", "claim_text": "SysML requirements tables and matrices facilitate requirements traceability, satisfaction, and verification by linking requirements to satisfying model elements and verifying test/model artifacts.", "evidence_confidence": "Moderate", "observed_effect": "Requirements are visibly connected to model elements and verifying artifacts, demonstrating model-based traceability on MASAT1 examples.", "source_claim_id": "C4", "source_path": "studies[15].extraction.claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[3]", "source_record_id": "paper-0023::claim-04", "study_id": "paper-0023"}</data>
    </edge>
