    </edge>
    <edge source="paper-0001" target="paper-0001::claim-01" id="e0001956">
      <data key="d5">HAS_CLAIM</data>
      <data key="d6">claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[]</data>
      <data key="d7">MBSE creates a system model that integrates discipline-specific engineering models/simulations and provides a cohesive, consistent source of requirements, design, analysis, and verification across development.</data>
      <data key="d8" />
      <data key="d9">MBSE creates a system model that helps integrate other discipline specific engineering models and simulations. The system level model is initiated at the start of a project and evolves throughout development. It provides a cohesive and consistent source of system requirements, design, analysis, and verification.</data>
      <data key="d10">high</data>
      <data key="d11">{"claim_id": "paper-0001::claim-01", "source_claim_id": "C1", "source_path": "studies[0].extraction.claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[0]", "source_record_id": "paper-0001::claim-01", "study_id": "paper-0001"}</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="paper-0001" target="paper-0001::claim-02" id="e0001957">
      <data key="d5">HAS_CLAIM</data>
      <data key="d6">claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[]</data>
      <data key="d7">The RAX executable MBSE representation integrates SysML architecture/behavior with MATLAB analytical models and STK simulation through ModelCenter/MBSE Analyzer, enabling execution of behavior and parametric models for subsystem functions/states.</data>
      <data key="d8" />
      <data key="d9">ModelCenter was used to integrate the analytical and simulation models. The integrated analyses were linked to the SysML model using MBSE Analyzer, a bridge between SysML tools and ModelCenter. Behavioral models were executed for a representative RAX mission to study energy state and data collection capabilities.</data>
      <data key="d10">high</data>
      <data key="d11">{"claim_id": "paper-0001::claim-02", "source_claim_id": "C2", "source_path": "studies[0].extraction.claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[1]", "source_record_id": "paper-0001::claim-02", "study_id": "paper-0001"}</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="paper-0001" target="paper-0001::claim-03" id="e0001958">
      <data key="d5">HAS_CLAIM</data>
      <data key="d6">claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[]</data>
      <data key="d7">Time-history simulation helps understand and verify coupled energy collection and data-download behavior in the CubeSat model.</data>
      <data key="d8" />
      <data key="d9">The time history plots show that the integrated RAX model correctly models the behavior of energy collection and data download, and their interactions.</data>
      <data key="d10">medium</data>
      <data key="d11">{"claim_id": "paper-0001::claim-03", "source_claim_id": "C3", "source_path": "studies[0].extraction.claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[2]", "source_record_id": "paper-0001::claim-03", "study_id": "paper-0001"}</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="paper-0001" target="paper-0001::claim-04" id="e0001959">
      <data key="d5">HAS_CLAIM</data>
      <data key="d6">claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[]</data>
      <data key="d7">Integrated MBSE enables verification of the impact of design decisions on mission requirements throughout the entire mission, beyond standalone STK/MATLAB analysis practice.</data>
      <data key="d8" />
      <data key="d9">In the current practice of satellite design, tools such as STK and MATLAB are used to analyze mission performance. However, there is no readily available capability that enables verifying the impact of design decisions on mission requirements throughout the entire mission. The integrated model demonstrates how to accomplish this by performing mission simulations using MBSE models.</data>
      <data key="d10">high</data>
      <data key="d11">{"claim_id": "paper-0001::claim-04", "source_claim_id": "C4", "source_path": "studies[0].extraction.claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[3]", "source_record_id": "paper-0001::claim-04", "study_id": "paper-0001"}</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="paper-0001" target="paper-0001::claim-05" id="e0001960">
      <data key="d5">HAS_CLAIM</data>
      <data key="d6">claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[]</data>
      <data key="d7">Solar-panel area trade study supports EPS design decision-making by showing nominal and half-size panels satisfy the energy requirement while quarter-size panels are too small.</data>
      <data key="d8" />
      <data key="d9">When the solar panel area was reduced further, to a quarter of the nominal, the energy level went below the required minimum at the end of the simulation... The results clearly show that the quarter size solar panel is too small for the mission.</data>
      <data key="d10">high</data>
      <data key="d11">{"claim_id": "paper-0001::claim-05", "source_claim_id": "C5", "source_path": "studies[0].extraction.claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[4]", "source_record_id": "paper-0001::claim-05", "study_id": "paper-0001"}</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="paper-0001" target="paper-0001::claim-06" id="e0001961">
      <data key="d5">HAS_CLAIM</data>
      <data key="d6">claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[]</data>
      <data key="d7">Battery-capacity trade study identifies energy spillover at reduced battery capacity and suggests possible solar-panel downsizing.</data>
      <data key="d8" />
      <data key="d9">When the battery capacity was reduced, energy level could not rise above the max capacity of 100,000 J. This shows that there will be spillover of energy collected by solar panel, and it may be considered to reduce the size of the solar panel.</data>
      <data key="d10">high</data>
      <data key="d11">{"claim_id": "paper-0001::claim-06", "source_claim_id": "C6", "source_path": "studies[0].extraction.claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[5]", "source_record_id": "paper-0001::claim-06", "study_id": "paper-0001"}</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="paper-0001" target="paper-0001::claim-07" id="e0001962">
      <data key="d5">HAS_CLAIM</data>
      <data key="d6">claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[]</data>
      <data key="d7">Orbit-altitude and ground-station network trade studies support mission design by estimating downloaded-data performance and checking data-downlink requirements.</data>
      <data key="d8" />
      <data key="d9">The nominal configuration using Ann Arbor and Menlo Park stations collected about 3.9 MB of data during 24 hours... Nonetheless, all three configurations satisfy the system requirement that 'the system shall download at least 1MB of data per day'.</data>
      <data key="d10">high</data>
      <data key="d11">{"claim_id": "paper-0001::claim-07", "source_claim_id": "C7", "source_path": "studies[0].extraction.claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[6]", "source_record_id": "paper-0001::claim-07", "study_id": "paper-0001"}</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="paper-0001" target="paper-0001::claim-08" id="e0001963">
      <data key="d5">HAS_CLAIM</data>
      <data key="d6">claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[]</data>
      <data key="d7">The RAX CubeSat model and manual are intended for academic release and can serve as a starting point for CubeSat teams to develop their own models and trade studies.</data>
      <data key="d8" />
      <data key="d9">The RAX CubeSat model will be made available to the academic community for further study and potential extension for more complex missions.</data>
      <data key="d10">high</data>
      <data key="d11">{"claim_id": "paper-0001::claim-08", "source_claim_id": "C8", "source_path": "studies[0].extraction.claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[7]", "source_record_id": "paper-0001::claim-08", "study_id": "paper-0001"}</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="paper-0001" target="paper-0001::claim-09" id="e0001964">
      <data key="d5">HAS_CLAIM</data>
      <data key="d6">claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[]</data>
      <data key="d7">The work demonstrates the power, scalability, and utility of available MBSE tools and methods for increasingly complex spacecraft missions.</data>
      <data key="d8" />
      <data key="d9">This work was undertaken to demonstrate the power, scalability, and utility of MBSE tools and methods that are available to help meet the challenge of designing spacecraft missions of ever-increasing complexity.</data>
      <data key="d10">medium</data>
      <data key="d11">{"claim_id": "paper-0001::claim-09", "source_claim_id": "C9", "source_path": "studies[0].extraction.claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[8]", "source_record_id": "paper-0001::claim-09", "study_id": "paper-0001"}</data>
    </edge>
