    <edge source="paper-0003" target="emergentcode::analysis_driven_model_minimization_rule" id="e0002348">
      <data key="d5">HAS_EMERGENT_CODE</data>
      <data key="d6">implementation_pathway_tailoring.tailoring_rationale; claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[].mbse_mechanism</data>
      <data key="d7">analysis-driven-model-minimization-rule</data>
      <data key="d8">The authors explicitly reduce model complexity by introducing model elements only when needed for an analysis or simulation; this is a lightweight-tailoring mechanism not directly enumerated.</data>
      <data key="d9">All modeling elements were introduced in the context of building or executing an analysis or simulation, which ensured they were required and minimized the overall complexity of the model.</data>
      <data key="d10">high</data>
      <data key="d11">{"category": "tailoring mechanism", "description": "The authors explicitly reduce model complexity by introducing model elements only when needed for an analysis or simulation; this is a lightweight-tailoring mechanism not directly enumerated.", "original_label": "Analysis-driven model-minimization rule", "source_path": "studies[2].emergent_codes[5]", "source_record_id": "paper-0003::emergent-06", "study_id": "paper-0003"}</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="paper-0003" target="emergentcode::develop_with_what_you_fly_with_dwwyfw" id="e0002349">
      <data key="d5">HAS_EMERGENT_CODE</data>
      <data key="d6">claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[].mbse_mechanism</data>
      <data key="d7">develop-with-what-you-fly-with-dwwyfw</data>
      <data key="d8">The paper frames full-lifecycle continuity using DWWYFW, which is not a controlled mechanism label.</data>
      <data key="d9">Using models to define, develop, and ultimately operate a system is known as "Develop With What You Fly With" (DWWYFW).</data>
      <data key="d10">high</data>
      <data key="d11">{"category": "method/philosophy", "description": "The paper frames full-lifecycle continuity using DWWYFW, which is not a controlled mechanism label.", "original_label": "Develop With What You Fly With (DWWYFW)", "source_path": "studies[2].emergent_codes[1]", "source_record_id": "paper-0003::emergent-02", "study_id": "paper-0003"}</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="paper-0003" target="emergentcode::no_magic_teamwork_google_collaboration_environment" id="e0002350">
      <data key="d5">HAS_EMERGENT_CODE</data>
      <data key="d6">governance_roi_project_decision_linkage.model_governance_strategy; claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[].mbse_mechanism</data>
      <data key="d7">no-magic-teamwork-google-collaboration-environment</data>
      <data key="d8">The paper reports distributed collaboration infrastructure but not formal governance controls; this is useful for synthesis on distributed CubeSat MBSE adoption.</data>
      <data key="d9">The collaborative environment includes a CubeSat - MBSE Google group, a MBSE Google documents collection, a No Magic Teamwork server for SysML modeling, and biweekly/weekly Web conferencing.</data>
      <data key="d10">high</data>
      <data key="d11">{"category": "collaboration infrastructure", "description": "The paper reports distributed collaboration infrastructure but not formal governance controls; this is useful for synthesis on distributed CubeSat MBSE adoption.", "original_label": "No Magic Teamwork/Google collaboration environment", "source_path": "studies[2].emergent_codes[6]", "source_record_id": "paper-0003::emergent-07", "study_id": "paper-0003"}</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="paper-0003" target="emergentcode::operational_schedule_robustness_as_a_future_mbse_use_case" id="e0002351">
      <data key="d5">HAS_EMERGENT_CODE</data>
      <data key="d6">eps_verification_specific.pre_physical_development_verification_mechanism; gap_mapping.lightweight_resource_constrained_tailoring</data>
      <data key="d7">operational-schedule-robustness-as-a-future-mbse-use-case</data>
      <data key="d8">The paper repeatedly links CubeSat operations to schedule feasibility and robustness, but efficient scheduling verification is future work rather than a controlled method category.</data>
      <data key="d9">it would be helpful to be able to assess the robustness of operational schedules to perturbations in various input parameters.</data>
      <data key="d10">high</data>
      <data key="d11">{"category": "future method/theme", "description": "The paper repeatedly links CubeSat operations to schedule feasibility and robustness, but efficient scheduling verification is future work rather than a controlled method category.", "original_label": "Operational schedule robustness as a future MBSE use case", "source_path": "studies[2].emergent_codes[7]", "source_record_id": "paper-0003::emergent-08", "study_id": "paper-0003"}</data>
    </edge>
