    <edge source="paper-0023" target="approachclass::extended_mbse" id="e0001661">
      <data key="d5">HAS_APPROACH_CLASS</data>
      <data key="d6">implementation_pathway_tailoring.approach_classification</data>
      <data key="d7">Extended MBSE</data>
      <data key="d8">The approach connects requirements, architecture, behavior, interfaces, verification, project breakdown deliverables, and lifecycle gates in SysML, but does not yet integrate multiple external engineering domains/tools at digital-engineering scale.</data>
      <data key="d9">Section 5/Fig. 11: PBS model elements are "organized ... into packages structured by both SysML diagram type and hierarchy concurrently"; packages include Requirements, Structure, Behavior, Verification, Parametric, and Launch Operation Facility.</data>
      <data key="d10">medium</data>
      <data key="d11">{"confidences": ["medium"], "source_path": null, "source_paths": ["studies[15].extraction.implementation_pathway_tailoring.approach_classification", "studies[15].structured_code_summary.implementation_pathway_tailoring.approach_classification"], "source_record_id": null, "source_record_ids": ["paper-0023::code-0057", "paper-0023::code-0195"], "source_sections": ["extraction", "structured_code_summary"], "status": "coded", "statuses": ["coded"], "study_id": "paper-0023", "value_index": 1}</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="paper-0023" target="paper-0023::claim-01" id="e0002069">
      <data key="d5">HAS_CLAIM</data>
      <data key="d6">claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[]</data>
      <data key="d7">A reusable MBSE framework/repository can guide limited-resource university teams through nanosatellite mission design and management, including mission analysis, design verification, and validation.</data>
      <data key="d8" />
      <data key="d9">Section 2: "The aim of using MBSE for a nanosatellite project is to create a model framework or repository that can be used by university teams (with limited resources) wishing to design CubeSat or Nanosatellite projects. This type of formal modeling application will guide them through the entire nanosatellite mission design and management process (mission analysis, system design verification, and validation)." Section 3.2/Fig. 4: the authors adopt "an MBSE Framework approach based on a model comprising a consistent set of views reflecting multiple viewpoints and stages in the nanosatellite project"; Fig. 4 shows Mission Analysis, System Formal Design, System Production &amp; Integration, and System Deployment &amp; Operation views.</data>
      <data key="d10">high</data>
      <data key="d11">{"claim_id": "paper-0023::claim-01", "source_claim_id": "C1", "source_path": "studies[15].extraction.claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[0]", "source_record_id": "paper-0023::claim-01", "study_id": "paper-0023"}</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="paper-0023" target="paper-0023::claim-02" id="e0002070">
      <data key="d5">HAS_CLAIM</data>
      <data key="d6">claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[]</data>
      <data key="d7">Tailoring ECSS/project-breakdown structures through MBSE simplifies academic nanosatellite project management by deriving schedule/CBS from the product tree and omitting industrial customer-supplier structures not needed for student projects.</data>
      <data key="d8" />
      <data key="d9">Section 3.1: "The project management procedures proposed by standards like ECSS are sometimes too over-specified for nanosatellite projects which are usually undertaken by universities or academic institutions." Section 3.2: academic nanosatellite specificities allow simplifying the structure: requirements are derived from mission objectives; budget is allocated to hardware/software; "schedule and Coast Breakdown Structure can be directly derived from the Product Tree"; "students-driven projects" have changing personnel and "Business Agreement Structure can be neglected." Section 3.4: outputs for the Formal Design Phase include "Product breakdown structure (PBS)", "Work breakdown structure (WBS)", and "Cost breakdown structure (CBS)"; CBS costs are primarily hardware/software development and testing for student-driven projects.</data>
      <data key="d10">high</data>
      <data key="d11">{"claim_id": "paper-0023::claim-02", "source_claim_id": "C2", "source_path": "studies[15].extraction.claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[1]", "source_record_id": "paper-0023::claim-02", "study_id": "paper-0023"}</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="paper-0023" target="paper-0023::claim-03" id="e0002071">
      <data key="d5">HAS_CLAIM</data>
      <data key="d6">claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[]</data>
      <data key="d7">MBSE/SysML improves mission reliability and provides traceability, design consistency, quality improvement, and reduced document-management overhead compared with traditional DBSE.</data>
      <data key="d8" />
      <data key="d9">Section 1: "The combination of Model-Based Systems Engineering (MBSE) and Systems Modeling Language (SysML) emerged as an alternative management and design approach that improves mission reliability ... Typical advantages of MBSE are that it provides better traceability, design consistency, quality improvement, and reduced document management overhead."</data>
      <data key="d10">high</data>
      <data key="d11">{"claim_id": "paper-0023::claim-03", "source_claim_id": "C3", "source_path": "studies[15].extraction.claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[2]", "source_record_id": "paper-0023::claim-03", "study_id": "paper-0023"}</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="paper-0023" target="paper-0023::claim-04" id="e0002072">
      <data key="d5">HAS_CLAIM</data>
      <data key="d6">claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[]</data>
      <data key="d7">SysML requirements tables and matrices facilitate requirements traceability, satisfaction, and verification by linking requirements to satisfying model elements and verifying test/model artifacts.</data>
      <data key="d8" />
      <data key="d9">Section 5.1: "requirements tables were generated ... These tables describe the requirements and their relationship to other SysML design elements to facilitate requirements traceability, satisfaction, and verification." Section 5.1/Fig. 15: "The "Satisfied By" column shows the model elements that fulfill the requirement. For example, the "Power System" subsystem must satisfy operational requirement 100.1.6."; "The 'Verified By' column shows the specific test cases that determine how the system meets the requirement ... "Bus System Activation" activity diagram ... is used to verify operational requirement 100.1.6." Section 5.1/Fig. 16: "The requirements matrix is another important SysML element that can be generated for requirements to design trace"; Fig. 16 shows a Mission Satisfy Requirement Matrix with Power System and Battery/Kill Switch rows.</data>
      <data key="d10">high</data>
      <data key="d11">{"claim_id": "paper-0023::claim-04", "source_claim_id": "C4", "source_path": "studies[15].extraction.claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[3]", "source_record_id": "paper-0023::claim-04", "study_id": "paper-0023"}</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="paper-0023" target="paper-0023::claim-05" id="e0002073">
      <data key="d5">HAS_CLAIM</data>
      <data key="d6">claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[]</data>
      <data key="d7">Package diagrams and model organization improve stakeholder understanding and help modelers build the nanosatellite model in a more organized way.</data>
      <data key="d8" />
      <data key="d9">Section 5: "Choosing to use package diagrams at this level of abstract modeling helps stakeholders understand the structure of the model and helps modelers build the model in a more organized way." Section 5/Fig. 11: PBS model elements are "organized ... into packages structured by both SysML diagram type and hierarchy concurrently"; packages include Requirements, Structure, Behavior, Verification, Parametric, and Launch Operation Facility.</data>
      <data key="d10">high</data>
      <data key="d11">{"claim_id": "paper-0023::claim-05", "source_claim_id": "C5", "source_path": "studies[15].extraction.claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[4]", "source_record_id": "paper-0023::claim-05", "study_id": "paper-0023"}</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="paper-0023" target="paper-0023::claim-06" id="e0002074">
      <data key="d5">HAS_CLAIM</data>
      <data key="d6">claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[]</data>
      <data key="d7">The LEOP flow and Bus System Activation behavior model verify an operational modes-transition requirement satisfied by the Power System before physical implementation.</data>
      <data key="d8" />
      <data key="d9">Section 5.1/Fig. 15: "The "Satisfied By" column shows the model elements that fulfill the requirement. For example, the "Power System" subsystem must satisfy operational requirement 100.1.6."; "The 'Verified By' column shows the specific test cases that determine how the system meets the requirement ... "Bus System Activation" activity diagram ... is used to verify operational requirement 100.1.6." Section 5.3: "The LEOP flow activity diagram is also a way to "verify" functional or operational requirements, such as the Bus System Activation state machine (Fig. 23), which verifies operational requirements 100.1.6." Fig. 23 description: Bus System Activation state machine uses LEOP Safe Mode (do/obc power on), LEOP Critical Mode, and Vbat &gt; Vsafe / Vbat &lt; Vcritic transitions to continue or recover the LEOP flow.</data>
      <data key="d10">medium</data>
      <data key="d11">{"claim_id": "paper-0023::claim-06", "source_claim_id": "C6", "source_path": "studies[15].extraction.claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[5]", "source_record_id": "paper-0023::claim-06", "study_id": "paper-0023"}</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="paper-0023" target="paper-0023::claim-07" id="e0002075">
      <data key="d5">HAS_CLAIM</data>
      <data key="d6">claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[]</data>
      <data key="d7">Implementing the MBSE view/process approach improved communication, planning, management, and development by replacing ad hoc work organization and facilitating PBS/WBS/CBS project documents.</data>
      <data key="d8" />
      <data key="d9">Conclusion: "To improve communication, planning, management, and development, we implemented this approach, replacing ad hoc procedures in the work organization with process flow and model based on the different views of the system architecture." Conclusion: "The proposed modeling also facilitated the development of key documents in a nanosatellite project, namely the product breakdown structure (PBS)" and "the PBS is considered as the backbone of the project design management activities allowing among other things to define the work breakdown structure (WBS) and the cost breakdown structure (CBS)."</data>
      <data key="d10">medium</data>
      <data key="d11">{"claim_id": "paper-0023::claim-07", "source_claim_id": "C7", "source_path": "studies[15].extraction.claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[6]", "source_record_id": "paper-0023::claim-07", "study_id": "paper-0023"}</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="paper-0023" target="paper-0023::claim-08" id="e0002076">
      <data key="d5">HAS_CLAIM</data>
      <data key="d6">claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[]</data>
      <data key="d7">Future integration with MATLAB/STK and executable SysML with a GUI is expected to improve viability/practicality and provide insight into nanosatellite behavior.</data>
      <data key="d8" />
      <data key="d9">Conclusion: "The next step of this work is to improve the viability and practicality of the product breakdown structure model by integrating external analysis and simulation tools such as MATLAB and STK. The aim is to develop an executable SysML model with a graphical user interface to provide insight into how nanosatellites work."</data>
