    <edge source="paper-0023" target="emergentcode::ecss_nasa_standards_tailoring_method" id="e0002453">
      <data key="d5">HAS_EMERGENT_CODE</data>
      <data key="d6">toolchain_interoperability_lifecycle_continuity.mbse_language_method_tool</data>
      <data key="d7">ecss-nasa-standards-tailoring-method</data>
      <data key="d8">The MBSE implementation is not only SysML tooling; it tailors ECSS project-management/engineering standards and NASA handbook guidance for academic nanosatellites. Controlled value other is used.</data>
      <data key="d9">Abstract: "The management and design process is based on a reference framework based on existing standards such as ESA's European Cooperation for Spatial Standardization (ECSS). The choice fell on the Systems Modeling Language (SysML) for the MBSE implementation with the future goal of developing executable SysML Modeling approaches."; Section 3.3: the space mission life cycle "must result in a deliverable document (review) that will evaluate and validate the work done and allow progress throughout the life cycle"; Fig. 5 lists MRR/SDR, PDR/CDR, SAR/PRR, ORR/DR.; Section 3.2: academic nanosatellite specificities allow simplifying the structure: requirements are derived from mission objectives; budget is allocated to hardware/software; "schedule and Coast Breakdown Structure can be directly derived from the Product Tree"; "students-driven projects" have changing personnel and "Business Agreement Structure can be neglected."</data>
      <data key="d10">high</data>
      <data key="d11">{"category": null, "description": "The MBSE implementation is not only SysML tooling; it tailors ECSS project-management/engineering standards and NASA handbook guidance for academic nanosatellites. Controlled value other is used.", "original_label": "ECSS/NASA standards-tailoring method", "source_path": "studies[15].emergent_codes[1]", "source_record_id": "paper-0023::emergent-02", "study_id": "paper-0023"}</data>
    </edge>
