    <edge source="paper-0033" target="paper-0033::claim-01" id="e0002110">
      <data key="d5">HAS_CLAIM</data>
      <data key="d6">claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[]</data>
      <data key="d7">The cyber-physical MAM plus MBSE toolchain enables visual configuration and verification/validation of CubeSat operations scenarios by modeling and simulation.</data>
      <data key="d8" />
      <data key="d9">From these graphs, we can infer that the power and data balances are stable, i.e. the satellite is capable of charging the batteries for the amount used each orbit, and download the generated mission data. This means, for this perspective, that this is a valid operation scenario.</data>
      <data key="d10" />
      <data key="d11">{"claim_id": "paper-0033::claim-01", "source_claim_id": "C1", "source_path": "studies[21].extraction.claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[0]", "source_record_id": "paper-0033::claim-01", "study_id": "paper-0033"}</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="paper-0033" target="paper-0033::claim-02" id="e0002111">
      <data key="d5">HAS_CLAIM</data>
      <data key="d6">claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[]</data>
      <data key="d7">Automatically generating the ForPlan configuration script from the MAM removes the need for users to understand simulator source code or manually code each scenario, making scenario changes more practical and faster.</data>
      <data key="d8" />
      <data key="d9">By using the MAM and automatically generating the input configuration script, the user is no longer required to understand how the simulator source code works, and does not have to manually code the configurations for each scenario, permitting a straightforward use of the simulator. Also, it is much more practical and faster to change scenario parameters in the model than manually in the configuration script</data>
      <data key="d10" />
      <data key="d11">{"claim_id": "paper-0033::claim-02", "source_claim_id": "C2", "source_path": "studies[21].extraction.claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[1]", "source_record_id": "paper-0033::claim-02", "study_id": "paper-0033"}</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="paper-0033" target="paper-0033::claim-03" id="e0002112">
      <data key="d5">HAS_CLAIM</data>
      <data key="d6">claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[]</data>
      <data key="d7">Iterative simulation results can drive changes in expected behavior or operation parameters until power and data budget requirements are met.</data>
      <data key="d8" />
      <data key="d9">The simulation results from each iteration are used to evaluate the amount of power used and data generated by each equipment, which are then used to drive changes in either expected behavior or operation parameters of each equipment</data>
      <data key="d10" />
      <data key="d11">{"claim_id": "paper-0033::claim-03", "source_claim_id": "C3", "source_path": "studies[21].extraction.claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[2]", "source_record_id": "paper-0033::claim-03", "study_id": "paper-0033"}</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="paper-0033" target="paper-0033::claim-04" id="e0002113">
      <data key="d5">HAS_CLAIM</data>
      <data key="d6">claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[]</data>
      <data key="d7">The generated simulations supported stakeholder discussions to reach a viable NCBR2 operation scenario and then guided flight software implementation.</data>
      <data key="d8" />
      <data key="d9">the resulting model was used to generate simulations for operations scenarios to be used in discussions among the stakeholders to reach a viable operation scenario</data>
      <data key="d10" />
      <data key="d11">{"claim_id": "paper-0033::claim-04", "source_claim_id": "C4", "source_path": "studies[21].extraction.claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[3]", "source_record_id": "paper-0033::claim-04", "study_id": "paper-0033"}</data>
    </edge>
