    <edge source="paper-0034" target="approachclass::pure_mbse" id="e0001668">
      <data key="d5">HAS_APPROACH_CLASS</data>
      <data key="d6">implementation_pathway_tailoring.approach_classification</data>
      <data key="d7">Pure MBSE</data>
      <data key="d8">The approach is centered on requirements models and RMM trace matrices with limited reported external analysis/simulation/tool integration; it is model-centered rather than a connected digital engineering environment.</data>
      <data key="d9">Requirements Modeling Language (RML) is a collection of diagrams used to model software from the business analysis or product management perspective [12]. RML is concerned with a project's goals and objectives, instead of complex system design models (which is the focus of UML and SySML) [13].</data>
      <data key="d10">medium</data>
      <data key="d11">{"confidences": ["medium"], "source_path": null, "source_paths": ["studies[22].extraction.implementation_pathway_tailoring.approach_classification", "studies[22].structured_code_summary.implementation_pathway_tailoring.approach_classification"], "source_record_id": null, "source_record_ids": ["paper-0034::code-0034", "paper-0034::code-0113"], "source_sections": ["extraction", "structured_code_summary"], "status": "coded", "statuses": ["coded"], "study_id": "paper-0034", "value_index": 1}</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="paper-0034" target="paper-0034::claim-01" id="e0002114">
      <data key="d5">HAS_CLAIM</data>
      <data key="d6">claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[]</data>
      <data key="d7">Applying RML to CubeSat/engineering-system development enhances requirements quality and eases analysis and elicitation by reducing miscommunication among heterogeneous stakeholders.</data>
      <data key="d8" />
      <data key="d9">Developing cyber-physical systems such as CubeSats requires dynamic engagements of stakeholders, developers, tester, analysts, and project managers. With their different backgrounds, the technicality of analyzing functional and nonfunctional requirements may lead to miscommunication between groups. Thus, applying requirements modeling language (RML) into an engineering system development project will enhance the quality of requirements as well as ease the analysis and elicitation processes.</data>
      <data key="d10">high</data>
      <data key="d11">{"claim_id": "paper-0034::claim-01", "source_claim_id": "C1", "source_path": "studies[22].extraction.claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[0]", "source_record_id": "paper-0034::claim-01", "study_id": "paper-0034"}</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="paper-0034" target="paper-0034::claim-02" id="e0002115">
      <data key="d5">HAS_CLAIM</data>
      <data key="d6">claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[]</data>
      <data key="d7">RMM traceability from requirements to process steps, code, and test cases helps ensure that every requirement is adequately tested.</data>
      <data key="d8" />
      <data key="d9">It works as a high-level documentation, which maps and traces user requirements with the corresponding test cases. This is intended to ensure that for each and every requirement there is an adequate level of testing successfully fulfilled.</data>
      <data key="d10">high</data>
      <data key="d11">{"claim_id": "paper-0034::claim-02", "source_claim_id": "C2", "source_path": "studies[22].extraction.claimed_outcomes_value_mechanisms_evidence[1]", "source_record_id": "paper-0034::claim-02", "study_id": "paper-0034"}</data>
    </edge>
