    <edge source="paper-0034" target="limitationflag::rml_is_not_full_sys_ml_or_eps_mbse" id="e0004135">
      <data key="d5">HAS_LIMITATION</data>
      <data key="d6">structured_limitations.emergent_limitations[]</data>
      <data key="d7">rml-is-not-full-sys-ml-or-eps-mbse</data>
      <data key="d8">RML is explicitly described as business/product-management-oriented and contrasted with UML/SysML system-design models, limiting transferability to full MBSE/EPS architecture modeling.</data>
      <data key="d9">Requirements Modeling Language (RML) is a collection of diagrams used to model software from the business analysis or product management perspective [12]. RML is concerned with a project's goals and objectives, instead of complex system design models (which is the focus of UML and SySML) [13].</data>
      <data key="d10">high</data>
      <data key="d11">{"assessment": "emergent_limitation", "limitation_type": "emergent_limitation", "source": null, "source_path": "studies[22].structured_limitations.emergent_limitations[1]", "source_record_id": "paper-0034::limitation-13", "study_id": "paper-0034"}</data>
    </edge>
